A||N||10||gtsonly||strut tower brace||||5||
Z||000000||gtsonly||08-11-2000||04:21 AM|||| hey all,<BR>  curious to know, if i were to make a strut tower brace, which ran from top to top, of each strut tower in the engine compartment would it work like a rear sway bar? you see these strut braces all the time on imports. i am thinking about constructing a proto-type, but i was wondering if it would be worth it or not.<P>   chris hatch||||reg||5
Z||000001||Standard||08-11-2000||06:01 AM||||From what I've heard, you really don't need one in back, because there's plenty of bracing on the trunk wall.  There's actually a guy up here in Minnesota that makes them, tho.  <P>Unless you're doing some serious racing, I don't think there's much to gain from putting one on.  Better off spending the time and money putting a sway bar on the rear if you haven't already.  just my .02 monetary units<P>------------------<BR>Mike -- Golden Valley, MN<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 148,000 miles and counting!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||gtsonly||08-11-2000||06:33 AM||||does he have a pic of it or is there a site to take a look?<BR>  chris hatch  ||||reg||14
Z||000003||1FST2M6||08-11-2000||07:59 AM||||take the trunk linning out of your car and you'll see the tube about 3"x1"x42" that connects the two strut towers. installing a brace works on a Civic cause there is nothing holding them together but on our cars like Standard said get a rear sway bar and you'll REALLY feel a difference.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Xtreme Fiero's||08-11-2000||08:07 AM||||I saw a Black 87 GT ("Devilish") at carlise it has a prety cool Chrome rear strut brace. I asked him where he got it and e said he made it hmi self, His wife said the same about the big ass cross drilled rotors though so i'm still looking to buy the brace from somewhere.||||reg||1
Z||000005||elfiero||08-11-2000||09:19 AM||||Xtreme,checkout the fiero big book under<BR>the MFF section you will find it listed there<BR>but,I have to agree-spend your hard earnd<BR>pittance on a rear swaybar! Just my two(.02)||||reg||2
Z||000006||2birds||08-11-2000||11:06 AM||||Rear anti-sway bar was around $80 from JC Whitney when I got mine.  Plan on another $20 for poly bushings and endlinks, and 3/8" bolts for the endlinks.  Makes a world of difference on my 86 GT.  <BR>The reason I don't think a strut tower brace does much for a Fiero is because of the aforementioned bracing across the back and front of the engine bay, making a nice solid square.  Front engine uni-body cars have the radiator support and core in the front, making a u-shaped front end, and a subframe that is bolted underneath that.<BR>I put one on my 97 T-bird, and it did wonders, so they do work for that type of setup.||||reg||1
Z||000007||1FST2M6||08-11-2000||11:51 AM||||if you have a pre-88 fiero cross drilled are easy to do... all you'll need is a drill press and a few hours. or you can buy them at <A HREF="http://www.cyberauto.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cyberauto.com</A>    the strut tower bar is also available at <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A>  ... I'd stay away from them... just added weight thats not helping you go fast or stop! <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 08-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Carrolles||08-11-2000||12:04 PM||||Randy Agee did a great article on mounting a Fiero front sway bar to the rear of a pre-88 Fiero.  <P>Go to:<BR> <A HREF="http://FieroZone.tripod.com/weekend/swaybar/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://FieroZone.tripod.com/weekend/swaybar/index.html</A>   <P>Carroll  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||HYPTNOTiSE||08-11-2000||01:11 PM||||Darrel Morse (MORSE86@aol.com) makes strut tower braces. i'm not sure how much but i know they look really cool<P>Mike LeCompte||||reg||1
Z||000010||Fformula88||08-11-2000||04:48 PM||||If your choice is between a sway bar and a strut tower brace, the sway bar is the way to go.  As everyone has pointed out, there is plenty of bracing in the Fiero engine compartment.  Its solid.  You will feel more of a performance gain out of the sway bar.  I also own a Ford Probe, and all those guys seem to recommend a front strut brace over a rear since it supports the wheels that steer.  Maybe they are full of hot air, and I probably shouldn't try to generalize between two very different cars, but they just don't think a rear is necessary except for VERY serious racers.  ||||reg||1
